{"name":"Georgian Revival Blue","hex":"#5b8d9f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"revival","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The act of reviving, or the state of being revived.","Reanimation from a state of languor or depression; applied to health, a person's spirits, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/revival"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Georgian architecture","blocks":[{"text":"Georgian architecture is the name given in most English-speaking countries to the set of architectural styles current between 1714 and 1830. It is named after the first four British monarchs of the House of Hanover, George I, George II, George III, and George IV, who reigned in continuous succession from August 1714 to June 1830."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/57_The_Close%2C_Salisbury.jpg/330px-57_The_Close%2C_Salisbury.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Georgian_architecture","wikidata":"Q1125300"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/The.circus.bath.arp.jpg/500px-The.circus.bath.arp.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/The.circus.bath.arp.jpg/1280px-The.circus.bath.arp.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/The.circus.bath.arp.jpg/500px-The.circus.bath.arp.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Very grand terrace houses at The Circus, Bath (1754), with basement \"areas\" and a profusion of columns.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe.circus.bath.arp.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Massachusetts_Hall%2C_Harvard_University.JPG/500px-Massachusetts_Hall%2C_Harvard_University.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Massachusetts_Hall%2C_Harvard_University.JPG/1280px-Massachusetts_Hall%2C_Harvard_University.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Massachusetts_Hall%2C_Harvard_University.JPG/500px-Massachusetts_Hall%2C_Harvard_University.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Function rules at Massachusetts Hall at Harvard University, 1718–20","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wik